  "articleBody": "It's easy to fall into traps when creating content for Google's \"People Also Ask\" (PAA) sections. While PAA is a fantastic visibility opportunity, many businesses make common blunders that actually hinder their chances of being cited. One frequent mistake is writing answers purely to capture search features without genuinely satisfying the user's underlying intent. If your content just skims the surface or feels like a keyword-stuffed response, AI systems and users alike will see right through it.\n\nAnother common pitfall is producing thin or repetitive answers. Just changing a few words around for similar questions won't cut it. Google's guidance emphasizes original, useful content created for readers first, not just for search engine manipulation. Copying competitor wording or making unsupported claims is also a big no-no; AI systems prioritize fact density and semantic relevance. You want your answers to be authoritative and unique.\n\nThen there's the issue of creating pages that overlap or compete with each other. This is called content cannibalization, and it can confuse both search engines and AI, making it harder for any single page to rank or be cited effectively. Each question should ideally have a distinct, direct answer supported by context, avoiding unnecessary duplication across your site. Also, not systematically targeting real questions from platforms like Quora or Reddit means you're potentially missing out on what prospects are genuinely curious about.\n\nCommon Mistakes to Avoid:\n- **Writing only for features:** Don't just chase snippets. Focus on truly helpful, comprehensive answers.\n- **Thin or repetitive content:** Ensure each answer is unique, fact-dense, and directly addresses the question.\n- **Copying competitors:** Originality and authenticity are key for AI systems and user trust.\n- **Unsupported claims:** Back up your statements with credible information and avoid exaggeration.\n- **Content cannibalization:** Make sure each piece of content has a clear purpose and doesn't compete with your other pages for the same intent.\n- **Ignoring real user questions:** Research beyond basic keyword tools to find out what people are *really* asking on Q&A sites.\n\nWhen Professional Guidance Helps:\nIf you're dealing with a large content library, technical products, or regulated industries, getting professional help for your PAA strategy can be incredibly beneficial. Experts can help audit existing content, identify true user intent, ensure accuracy, and structure your information in a way that AI systems can easily understand and cite, preventing these common and costly mistakes.",
